Spec: Core Layer Architecture
Purpose: Defines the overall system architecture, components, and IPC rules for the core-layer.

Decisions:
  Embedded Supervisor Pattern: Although the core components are decoupled isolated binaries (communicating only via UDS and not sharing RAM), they are required to run inside a single Docker container. To conform to Docker's PID 1 requirement without introducing external tools like supervisord or s6-overlay, the `http-server` acts as an embedded supervisor. It natively spawns the `iam-server` and `scheduler-server` as child processes and correctly delegates graceful shutdown signals (SIGTERM) to them.

Must:
  The core-layer is composed of strongly decoupled microservices communicating over Unix Domain Sockets (UDS).
  The central communication hub is the PostgreSQL database, managed efficiently via sqlx connection pools.
  The core components run inside a single container.
  The scheduler-server acts as the central job orchestrator, orchestrates nano-services, and manages task queues.
  The http-server provides the REST API, static content, and health check.
  The http-server validates incoming requests via IAM and delegates workflow operations to the scheduler.
  The iam-server handles authentication (session tokens) and authorization (RBAC policies) and runs independently.
  The UDS base path can be controlled via an ENV variable.
  The http-server generates a unique Request-ID (UUID) for each incoming request.
  This Request-ID is passed along via UDS payloads to the iam-server and scheduler-server to enable consolidated logging.

Must not:
  The iam-server must not communicate directly with the scheduler-server.
